---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { COMPANION_NAME, COMPANION_URL, SITE_URL } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="Source tier hierarchy, confidence ratings, verification discipline, and the open questions under verification for the Corrections Pest Reference."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Methodology', url: `${SITE_URL}/methodology/` }]}
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>

  <p>
    Every fact published on this reference is sourced to a primary authority and rated
    for source confidence. This page documents the source tier hierarchy, the confidence
    ratings, the verification discipline, and the questions that are open and under
    verification. The same methodology governs the companion
    <a href={COMPANION_URL}>{COMPANION_NAME}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Source Tier Hierarchy</h2>

  <h3>Tier 1 — Authoritative</h3>

  <p>
    Statutes and regulations (the Code of Federal Regulations, state administrative
    codes); the published standards and policies of the agencies that operate or govern
    correctional and detention facilities (for example, U.S. Immigration and Customs
    Enforcement, the Federal Bureau of Prisons, the U.S. Marshals Service, and state
    jail standards bodies and departments of corrections); federal and state regulators
    such as OSHA, FDA, EPA, CDC, and state departments of health and agriculture;
    published court opinions; and the Entomological Society of America Certification
    Corporation for matters relating to the Board Certified Entomologist credential.
    Tier 1 sources are quoted directly.
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 2 — Recognized Authority</h3>

  <p>
    Accrediting and professional bodies (for example, the American Correctional
    Association and the National Commission on Correctional Health Care), NIOSH, the
    National Institute of Corrections, and peer-reviewed literature in entomology,
    public health, and correctional health. Where a Tier 2 standard is licensed or
    paywalled, it is recorded by identifier, title, edition, and publisher only, and
    its text is not reproduced.
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 3 — Reference Only</h3>

  <p>
    News coverage, trade press, law-firm summaries, and vendor material. Tier 3 sources
    are used only to locate a Tier 1 or Tier 2 source to verify against. A claim that
    exists only in Tier 3 material is not published as a stated fact.
  </p>

  <h2>Source Confidence Ratings</h2>

  <h3>HIGH Confidence</h3>

  <p>
    The verbatim text has been read from the issuing body's own publication and checked
    by extraction or quotation. Page metadata gives the source URL and the date of the
    most recent verification.
  </p>

  <h3>MEDIUM Confidence</h3>

  <p>
    The primary source exists but could not be read directly from the issuing body, and
    was read from an official mirror or a documented capture. Where MEDIUM confidence
    applies, the page discloses the limitation.
  </p>

  <h3>KILLED — Investigated and Disconfirmed</h3>

  <p>
    The claim was investigated against primary sources and found to be fabricated,
    mis-cited, or supported only by Tier 3 material. Killed claims are not published as
    facts; they are catalogued so they are not reintroduced.
  </p>

  <h2>Investigated and Excluded Claims</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"7 CFR Part 110 federal recordkeeping requirements for restricted-use
      pesticides remain in effect."</strong> Disconfirmed. USDA rescinded 7 CFR Part 110
      effective July 11, 2025 (Federal Register Vol. 90 No. 90, May 12, 2025, FR Doc
      2025-08220). State-level recordkeeping rules now govern; for Utah, see
      <a href="/authorities/utah-r68-7-pesticide-applicator/">R68-7-11(11)</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Utah's Category 7 pesticide applicator definition names 'institutions
      such as schools, hospitals.'"</strong> Disconfirmed. R68-7-7(7) lists "dwelling,
      educational institution, or medical institution" and, separately, "prison."
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Page-specific disconfirmations appear on the authority page they concern.
  </p>

  <h2 id="open-questions">Open Questions Under Verification</h2>

  <p>
    Until a question is closed against a primary source, no page on this reference
    asserts an answer to it. Closed questions are listed with the date and the page
    that now carries the answer.
  </p>

  <h3>Still open</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>OSHA coverage of federal employers.</strong> How OSHA requirements reach the
      staff of a federal detention facility. (Coverage of Utah's state and local public
      employers is closed; see below.)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Food code reach outside Utah.</strong> For each state other than Utah, whether
      jail and prison kitchens are inspected under the adopted food code, and by which agency.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Federal Bureau of Prisons occupational safety and health policy.</strong> The
      current number and pest control text of the "National Occupational Safety and Health"
      program statement that the Food Service Manual refers to. (The Food Service Manual
      question is closed; see below.)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Utah county jails holding no state inmates.</strong> What standards those jails
      operate under, what part is public, and what the public text says about pests and
      sanitation. (The Utah Department of Corrections standards for secure facilities
      housing state inmates are published; see below.)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>General-use pesticides applied by facility staff in Utah.</strong> How UDAF
      reads the first sentence of R68-7-9(1)(a) for a public employee applying general-use
      products in the employer's own building. (The restricted use pesticide question is
      closed; see below.)
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Closed September 24, 2026</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>OSHA coverage of Utah public employers.</strong> Utah's State Plan covers all
      state and local government workers, and Utah Admin. Code R614-1-4.A.3 incorporates
      29 CFR 1910.21 through the end of part 1910, which includes 1910.141. See
      <a href="/authorities/osha-1910-141-sanitation/">the OSHA page</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Food code reach in Utah.</strong> R392-100 incorporates the 2022 Food Code
      without excluding Section 6-501.111, its exemption list does not name jails or prisons,
      and UDC Standard G-05 requires an annual health inspection of a secure facility's food
      service area. See <a href="/authorities/utah-r392-100-food-service-sanitation/">R392-100</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Current Bureau of Prisons Food Service Manual.</strong> Program Statement
      4700.08, May 7, 2026, which rescinds 4700.07. See
      <a href="/authorities/bop-p4700-08-food-service-manual/">P4700.08</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Applicability of ICE's detention standards.</strong> Stated by ICE in the
      PBNDS 2011 scope sections and the NDS 2019 overview. See the four ICE pages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restricted use pesticides applied by facility staff in Utah.</strong>
      R68-7-12(1)(a) requires a non-commercial applicator license for applying restricted
      use pesticides for a city, county, state, or federal government entity. See
      <a href="/authorities/utah-r68-7-pesticide-applicator/">R68-7</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Verification Discipline</h2>

  <p>
    Every claim is treated as unverified until it has been read in the primary source.
    Quotations are reproduced verbatim with their location. A page publishes only what
    its source says; where a source is silent on a question a reader will ask, the page
    says the question is open rather than supplying an answer.
  </p>

  <p>
    New authority pages are added only after primary-source verification. Existing pages
    are re-verified at least annually or upon any reported change to the underlying
    authority, and the page's verification date is updated only when a verification has
    actually been performed.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
